<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=scissors">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scissors</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Shears</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scissors are hand-operated cutting tools made of a pair of pivoting blades whose sharpened edges slide past each other. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> shears with a bow, the older and simpler form, appear around 1000 BCE, and seem to have been rare because few tasks were done better with them than with a knife. Scissors with a pivot became common in north-west Europe in the 13th and 14th centuries CE. In 1761 Robert Hinchliffe of Sheffield produced the first pair made of cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> for the mass market. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> Zhang Xiaoquan Company has made pivoted scissors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> since 1663.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scissors.jpg" alt="Scissors" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doggerelblogger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_household_scissors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
